ghpull:584 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "shacl: batch sh:sparql SELECT-constraint evaluation across a shape's focus nodes" ;
    bl:number 584 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:521 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr584 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:521 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-13T22:23:27+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-21T22:22:01Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr584Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #521 was explicitly speculative: per-focus-node sh:sparql SELECT execution (one query parse + BGP evaluation per node, via a single-row VALUES ($this)) was chosen for spec correctness in #518, but the issue only tracked the tradeoff without confirming batching was worth the added complexity. Before implementing anything, ran an ad hoc benchmark: 1000 focus nodes with a simple sh:sparql sh:select constraint, comparing shacl::validate's existing per-node loop against a hand-built single execution with a 1000-row VALUES block. Per-node took ~157ms vs ~11ms batched (about 14x), confirming the per-node re-parse (nom parsing 1000x) and per-execution BGP-evaluation setup cost is real, not just theoretical. Implemented is_batchable() in shacl/src/sparql_constraints.rs: unsafe whenever LIMIT/OFFSET is present, or an aggregate exists with no GROUP BY (implicit single group over the whole batched set), or GROUP BY is non-empty but doesn't include $this (rows from different focus nodes could merge into one group even without an aggregate). Safe when GROUP BY includes $this, since every group is then scoped to exactly one node. The batched path (eval_batched_select) force-projects $this if the query doesn't already select it or use SELECT *, injects one multi-row VALUES, executes once, and re-splits rows by their $this binding using a GraphElement->GraphElementId map built from the shape's focus nodes. sh:ask constraints are excluded by design (no per-row $this to split an ASK's single boolean by). Five tests were written first (#[ignore]d): batched correctness with 200 nodes, LIMIT and OFFSET each correctly falling back to per-node with identical semantics, an ungrouped aggregate falling back, and GROUP BY $this correctly using the batched path with the same results as the ungrouped case. All five pass after implementation; the workspace's full quality gate (fmt/clippy/shacl_suite) is clean, and CI is green (a local sparql_endpoint query_timeout SIGKILL was confirmed environment-specific — CPU contention from other concurrent agent sessions on the shared machine — and unrelated to this change)." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:584 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr584 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr584Summary agp:decisionPoint session:pr584Decision .

session:pr584Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Chose to only add the batched fast path when the top-level Select clause has no LIMIT/OFFSET and, if it has an aggregate, GROUP BY includes $this as one of the grouping keys — everything else keeps the original per-node loop unchanged rather than trying to make every case batchable." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered also batching sh:ask constraints by attaching a synthetic $this-labelled boolean column, but rejected it: ASK has no row-level output to split by $this at all in SPARQL semantics, so faking one would be a non-obvious rewrite of the query's own form rather than a straightforward VALUES/re-split, and the issue itself scoped the request to SELECT constraints only." .
